# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for gtopt.
#
# Used by .github/workflows/tsan.yml and by local TSan runs (see the
# "Sanitizers" section of CLAUDE.md):
#
#   TSAN_OPTIONS="suppressions=$PWD/tools/tsan.supp halt_on_error=0 \
#     second_deadlock_stack=1 history_size=4" ctest ...
#
# Rules for adding an entry:
#   * Never suppress a race whose frames are only in gtopt code.  Fix it,
#     or (for a known issue being fixed elsewhere) add a narrow entry that
#     names the branch/PR fixing it, and delete the entry once it lands.
#   * Third-party libraries are NOT instrumented, so TSan cannot see their
#     internal synchronisation (lock-free atomics, futexes, custom
#     allocators) and reports the memory they hand back to us as racy.
#     Suppress those by library module or by the library's own symbols,
#     never with a pattern that also matches gtopt code.
#   * `race:X` matches X against the function, file and module name of
#     every frame of BOTH stacks of a report, so a module pattern such as
#     `libhighs.so` hides any report in which that library takes part.
#   * Every entry carries a justification.

# ---- HiGHS (libhighs.so.1) -------------------------------------------------
# HiGHS runs its own work-stealing task executor (HighsTaskExecutor): the
# gtopt HiGHS plugin requests `threads = physical cores`, so every solve
# spawns HiGHS worker threads (pthread_create from inside Highs::run) that
# copy HighsLp / HighsOptions, run symmetry detection, sub-MIPs, etc. and
# park in run_worker().  All hand-offs between the calling thread and those
# workers go through lock-free atomics inside the uninstrumented libhighs,
# so TSan sees: ~HighsLp vs HighsLp copy, ~HighsSymmetryDetection vs
# createNode, HighsOptions::operator= vs memcpy, executor dispose() vs
# run_worker's pthread_cond_wait, and worker reads of the caller's stack
# during LinearInterface::clone.  In every observed report one side is a
# HiGHS-internal worker thread.  The module pattern deliberately does NOT
# match gtopt's own plugin (libgtopt_solver_highs.so).
race:libhighs.so

# ---- dynamic TLS of dlopen()ed solver plugins ------------------------------
# HighsSolverBackend::apply_options keeps a `static thread_local int` in the
# dlopen()ed plugin.  glibc allocates that dynamic TLS block with malloc and
# frees it from _dl_deallocate_tls (uninstrumented ld.so) when a work-pool
# thread exits; when another thread's TLS block later reuses the address
# TSan reports a race between the free and the new thread's first access.
# Per-thread data, no sharing: known TSan false positive with dynamic TLS.
race:_dl_deallocate_tls

# ---- Apache Arrow / Parquet I/O stack --------------------------------------
# Arrow's CSV/Parquet readers complete work on Arrow's internal CPU thread
# pool and publish results through arrow::Future (atomics inside libarrow);
# Arrow allocates through its bundled mimalloc pool, whose lock-free
# cross-thread free lists are invisible to TSan; function-local statics in
# libarrow (arrow::float32(), uint16(), Fingerprintable) are guarded by the
# uninstrumented libarrow's __cxa_guard.  Observed reports: csv_read_table /
# load_boundary_cuts_csv / boundary_cut_coeff_stats / load_cuts reading a
# Table that an Arrow pool thread built, and concurrent
# OutputContext::write / parquet writers (one file per work-pool task, no
# shared writer object) racing only inside mimalloc / zstd / zlib buffers
# reused across threads.
race:libarrow.so
race:libparquet.so
race:libmimalloc.so
race:libzstd.so
race:libz.so.1
